WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.763
Unit 3 is drift.

2
00:00:01.763 --> 00:00:04.464
A drift event is a
regression between two runs,

3
00:00:04.464 --> 00:00:09.100
and the book treats each one as a compliance
violation, not an advisory signal.

4
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:14.407
The kit's comparison returns a row
for a new usable Windows or SQL login,

5
00:00:14.407 --> 00:00:19.887
an Entra login removed, S A re-enabled,
SQL authentication accepted again,

6
00:00:19.887 --> 00:00:24.063
remote N T L M back after none,
or an instance not seen before.

7
00:00:24.063 --> 00:00:28.337
It applies the exception registry, so
the break-glass group isn't drift.

8
00:00:28.337 --> 00:00:32.691
The Arc agent going offline changes
nothing in the audit, so the comparison

9
00:00:32.691 --> 00:00:36.624
reports Arc regressed only when you
give it both runs' Arc snapshots.

10
00:00:36.624 --> 00:00:40.375
And a mode changed in the registry
but not yet restarted is invisible,

11
00:00:40.375 --> 00:00:42.612
because the audit reads the running mode.

12
00:00:42.612 --> 00:00:46.099
Runbook R nine adds a registry
check of login mode for that.

13
00:00:46.099 --> 00:00:48.988
Each row is a finding: open a task for it.

14
00:00:48.988 --> 00:00:53.210
The workbook Building Ticket-to-Machine
turns a drift row into a typed task.
